{{History of Balisca}}

'''Baliscan post-revolutionary social policy''' refers to the comprehensive transformation of social relations, cultural norms, and state institutions that occurred during the [[Processo Revolucionario en Curso]] ('''PREC''';  {{Literal translation|Ongoing Revolutionary Process}}) from 1919 to 1929. 

Following the [[Baliscan Revolution|revolution]], Balisca experienced a decade of radical social experimentation characterized by [[W:Anti-clericalism|anti-clericalism]], [[W:Gender emancipation|gender emancipation]], [[W:Indigenous decolonization|indigenous decolonization]], educational expansion, and the systematic dismantling of traditional hierarchies. Aiming to overcome [[W:Backlash (sociology)|conservative social backlash]], the PREC represented a sustained effort to implement [[W:Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist]] and [[W:Cultural liberalism|culturally liberal]] principles in daily life through mass participation, popular assemblies, and institutional transformation.

== The post-revolutionary demographic transformation ==
{{Main|1919 Confederal Convention}}
[[File:Baliscan post-revolution migrants to the Levante.webp|left|thumb|330x330px|Following the [[PREC|consolidation of the revolutionary regime]] and abolition of internal borders, there was an unprecedented migration wave to urban areas. Pictured: Migrants cross the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] on their way to [[Concepción]] ([[Arcadia]], {{Circa|1923}})]]
The conclusion of the revolutionary violence in 1919 initiated a profound demographic restructuring of Baliscan society. The abolition of internal borders—formalized at the [[1919 Confederal Convention]]—enabled unprecedented population mobility, while the collectivization of agriculture and worker control of industry redistributed populations from rural estates to urban centers and [[People's communes|cooperative communities]]. The ''población trashumante'' ("wandering population") became a defining phenomenon of the period, as hundreds of thousands of former agricultural laborers, domestic servants, and unemployed workers migrated toward the revolutionary communes and worker-controlled factories of the [[Baliscan Levante]] and the [[Marañón basin]].

This internal migration was accompanied by the departure of significant segments of the former economic and political elite—the ''flight of capital and talent'' noted by contemporary observers. Landowners, industrialists, clergy, and professionals who opposed the revolutionary order emigrated abroad, creating both a ''brain drain'' crisis and opportunities for previously marginalized groups to assume positions of responsibility. Women, indigenous peoples, and working-class Baliscans entered professions, administrative positions, and educational institutions that were previously monopolized by the pre-revolutionary elite (like the [[Volisan aristocracy]]).

The period also witnessed the acceleration of urbanization. The population of [[Macaio]] and [[Concepción]] grew by approximately 40 percent between 1919 and 1929, while secondary cities such as [[Uxiu]], [[Yerba Buena]], and [[Merced]] experienced slower but still comparable growth. This urban expansion was not merely demographic but spatial; the expropriation of elite properties and church holdings provided housing for the incoming population, while new construction—much of it cooperative or communally owned—established the distinctive ''barrios revolucionarios'' (revolutionary neighborhoods) that remain characteristic of Baliscan urban geography.

== Aspects of post-revolutionary social policy ==

=== Religion and anti-clericalism ===
{{Main|Basic Law: Freedom of Religious Belief|Religion in Balisca}}
The PREC implemented one of the most thoroughgoing programs of [[W:Secularization|secularization]] in modern history, with revolutionaries identifying the [[Petran Catholic Church|Catholic Church]] as a pillar of the [[W:Ancien régime|''ancien régime'']], complicit in the maintenance of hierarchical social relations and the ideological legitimation of exploitation. [[W:Anti-clericalism|Anti-clericalism]] thus became not merely a policy but a revolutionary imperative, pursued through both state action and [[W:Mass mobilization|popular mobilization]].

Church properties were expropriated without compensation and converted to secular uses: churches became schools, community centers, warehouses, and in some cases worker housing; monasteries and convents were transformed into hospitals, orphanages, and cooperative workshops; church lands were collectivized along with other large estates. The ''Ley de Secularización'' of 1920 formally [[W:Seperation of church and state|separated church and state]], abolishing [[W:Religious education|religious education]] in public schools, eliminating religious oaths and  religious influence in public spaces, and establishing civil marriage and divorce as the only legally recognized forms of union.

The revolutionary government actively promoted secular alternatives to religious practice. The ''Fiestas de la Razón'' (Festivals of Reason) replaced religious holidays with celebrations commemorating revolutionary events, scientific achievement, and human solidarity. The ''Escuelas Laicas'' network provided education explicitly oriented toward [[W:Rationalism|rationalist]] and [[W:Secular humanism|secular humanist]] values, rejecting the catechetical instruction that had characterized pre-revolutionary schooling. Popular anti-clerical organizations, including workers' committees and [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] youth groups, organized demonstrations, published satirical newspapers, and in some cases participated in the destruction of religious iconography—actions that generated both domestic controversy and international condemnation.

However, the anti-clerical program encountered significant resistance. In rural communities where Catholic practice remained central to social life, the implementation of secularization provoked tension between revolutionary cadres and local populations. The [[Mozbayana|Mozbayán]] highlands and the indigenous communities of the [[Marañón basin]]—where religious practice had syncretized with indigenous cosmologies—proved particularly resistant to top-down secularization. The revolutionary government eventually adopted a more flexible approach in these regions, permitting limited religious practice while maintaining institutional separation between church and state.

=== Gender roles and women's emancipation ===
{{Main|Women's rights in Balisca|Sex education in Balisca}}

The PREC transformed gender relations more thoroughly than any prior period in Baliscan history. Building on women's participation in the revolutionary struggle itself—the ''mujeres combatientes'' who had served in militias, organized strikes, and maintained logistical networks—the post-revolutionary period institutionalized gender equality through law, education, and economic restructuring.

The [[Confederal Decree on Sexual Education]] and [[Confederal Decree on Reproductive Autonomy]], both issued in 1921 following the ''[[Silenciamiento scandal]]'', established comprehensive sex education and legalized abortion throughout the confederation—among the earliest such measures globally. These policies reflected the revolutionary understanding that women's liberation required both economic independence and control over reproductive capacity. The sex education curriculum explicitly addressed the patriarchal structures that had enabled systematic exploitation, presenting sexual autonomy as inseparable from broader social emancipation.

Economic policy promoted women's entry into the formal workforce. The expropriation of domestic service households—which had employed millions of women as live-in servants—was accompanied by the establishment of cooperative childcare facilities, communal kitchens, and laundries. These ''servicios socializados'' (socialized services) aimed to free willing women from [[W:Domestic labor|domestic labor]], expanding participation in [[W:Worker council|worker councils]], educational programs, and political assemblies. The slogan ''"La mujer en la fábrica, el niño en la guardería, la familia en la comunidad"'' ("The woman in the factory, the child in the nursery, the family in the community") encapsulated the revolutionary ideal of collectivized domestic responsibility.

Legal equality was formalized through the ''Código de Familia'' of 1923, which established equal rights for women in marriage, divorce, property ownership, and [[W:Child custody|child custody]]. The criminalization of [[W:Domestic violence|domestic violence]]—the first such legislation in the Western Hemisphere—reflected the revolutionary commitment to transforming the ''private'' sphere that had traditionally been considered beyond state intervention. Women's organizations, including the feminist unions that had emerged during the revolutionary struggle, participated actively in the implementation of these policies, staffing counseling centers, legal aid offices, and educational campaigns.

=== Indigenous decolonization ===

{{Main|Indigenous decolonization in Balisca|Indigenous nations in Balisca}}

The PREC accelerated the process of indigenous decolonization initiated by the [[1883 Confederal Convention]]. The revolutionary government recognized that the formal equality established in the 19th century had failed to address the material and cultural legacies of colonialism—land dispossession, linguistic suppression, and the systematic marginalization of indigenous communities. [[W:Indigenous decolonization|Indigenous decolonization]] would emerge as a central component of the broader revolutionary program, particularly when it came to [[W:Indigenous land rights|land rights]], [[W:Indigenous planning|planning]] and intercommunal [[Reconciliation council (Balisca)|reconciliation]]. The expropriation of large agricultural estates included attempts to restore the collective landholdings to indigenous communities where it was possible. The ''restitution despoliada'' (dispossessed restitution) program returned ancestral territories to [[Wallche people|Wallche]], [[Kurupiranga people|Kurupirangan]], and other indigenous nations, establishing communal ownership structures that precluded individual alienation. Indigenous governance systems—including the ''lof'' councils of the Wallche and the ''oka'' assemblies of the Kurupiranga—received formal recognition as administrative authorities within the confederal structure.

Inclusive educational standards were progressively adopted at the confederal level from 1922, first encouraging bilingual instruction in indigenous languages in the territories that they historically inhabited, and later with indigenous history and culture being incorporated into the [[Confederal Academic Baseline Initiative]]. The ''Escuelas Indígenas'' network employed indigenous educators, developed curricula incorporating traditional knowledge systems, and rejected the assimilationist pedagogy that had characterized pre-revolutionary indigenous education across the confederation. The [[Kuarupí language]] received official recognition alongside Baliscano in the confederal capital, while the long-marginalized [[Yamanakuta language]] of the western [[Cerdanya|Cerdanyan]] (''Lapãyãla'') indigenous communities achieved comparable status in their ancestral lands.

However, the revolutionary approach to indigenous decolonization generated tensions, especially within rural communities, and between the [[Left-wing politics in Balisca|various currents of socialist principles at play in the country]]. The preservation of traditional governance structures and collective landholding arrangements conflicted with the revolutionary ideal of freely associating individuals cooperating through voluntary councils. Some revolutionary cadres viewed indigenous [[W:Communalism|communalism]] as [[W:Proto-socialism|proto-socialist]] and therefore compatible with the broader program; others regarded it as a particularist survival requiring eventual transcendence. The resolution of this tension—through the concept of ''pluralismo revolucionario'' (revolutionary pluralism)—established the framework for indigenous autonomy that persists in contemporary Balisca.

=== Education and culture ===

The PREC transformed [[Education in Balisca|education]] from a mechanism of [[W:Social reproduction|social reproduction]] into an instrument of revolutionary transformation. The expropriation of church schools and elite private academies, combined with a massive increase inn investment in public education, expanded access beyond anything previously witnessed in Balisca. Literacy campaigns—the ''Campañas de Alfabetización''—targeted adults excluded from formal schooling under the pre-revolutionary order, employing volunteer teachers in a program consciously modeled on the revolutionary pedagogy popularized during the insurrectionary period.

Curricula were revised to emphasize revolutionary values, critical thinking, and scientific rationalism. The ''método dialéctico'' (dialectical method) encouraged students to question received wisdom rather than memorize authoritative texts. History instruction was reoriented from the celebration of national heroes to the analysis of class struggle, colonialism, and popular resistance. The arts were incorporated as essential components of education; revolutionary cultural policy held that aesthetic development was inseparable from political consciousness.

The period witnessed a flourishing of proletarian and revolutionary culture. The ''Teatro Obrero'' (Workers' Theater) movement produced plays dramatizing class conflict and revolutionary struggle, performed in union halls, community centers, and vacant churches. ''Canto revolucionario'' (revolutionary song) emerged as a distinctive musical genre, disseminated through ''orfeones obreros'' (workers' choral societies) and performed at demonstrations, festivals, and educational events. Visual artists associated with the ''Vanguardia Socialista'' produced murals, posters, and public art celebrating revolutionary themes—work that significantly influenced the development of [[Socialist realism in Balisca|Baliscan socialist realism]] and the later [[Cosmocazo]] architectural movement.

The expansion of higher education accompanied these broader transformations. The expropriation of private universities and their integration into the public system democratized access to professional training. Women and working-class students enrolled in universities in unprecedented numbers, while curricula in medicine, engineering, and the social sciences were reoriented toward addressing the needs of the popular classes. The [[University of Concordia]], reorganized as a public institution in 1921, became a center of revolutionary intellectual production, attracting radical scholars from across the world.

=== Sexuality and family ===
{{Main|LGBTQ rights in Balisca|Marriage in Balisca}}

The PREC transformed sexual and family relations through a combination of legislative reform, educational initiative, and ideological reorientation. The sex education program established in 1921 addressed not only biological reproduction but also the social construction of gender, the history of patriarchy, and the revolutionary potential of transformed intimate relations. Instruction explicitly rejected the nuclear family as a "bourgeois institution" while emphasizing that new forms of collectivity must replace rather than simply destroy traditional arrangements.

The legalization of divorce—implemented through the ''Ley de Divorcio'' of 1920—made Balisca one of the first countries to permit dissolution of marriage by mutual consent. The legislation explicitly framed divorce as a mechanism of women's liberation, noting that the tying of material conditions to marriage had served to enforce the subjugation of women and enable domestic exploitation. Civil marriage registries were established throughout the confederation, replacing religious ceremonies as the only legally recognized form of union.
[[File:Llanito drag ball 1920s .jpg|left|thumb|Two Black patrons of an early [[W:Cross-dressing ball|drag ball]] in [[Castejón]]'s [[Llanito]] ({{Circa|1923}}) ]]
The period witnessed increased visibility of same-sex relationships and gender nonconformity. While homosexuality had never been explictly illegal at any point in Baliscan history, homosexuals, [[Gender nonconforming traditions in Balisca|gender non-conforming]] and other queer individuals were often selectively targeted by "public morality" ordinances. While legalized during the PREC—the criminal codes inherited from the pre-revolutionary period were simply not enforced—revolutionary discourse increasingly framed sexual diversity as compatible with, even expressive of, the broader emancipatory project. Anarchist publications (including [[La Voz de la Mujer|''La Voz de la Mujer'']]) discussed same-sex desire as a legitimate form of free association, while the cultural experimentation of the period permitted limited public expression of previously taboo identities. The later [[LGBTQ rights in Balisca|LGBTQ rights movement]] would claim the PREC as a foundational moment in the history of [[W:Sexual liberation|sexual liberation]].

The transformation of family structure was accompanied by new institutional arrangements. Communal child-rearing—implemented through cooperative crèches and youth houses—reduced the intensity of parental authority while ensuring children received revolutionary socialization. Communal kitchens and dining halls permitted, though did not require, the collectivization of domestic labor. The tension between these arrangements and the persistence of affective family bonds generated ongoing debate; popular practice eventually stabilized around a ''familia ampliada'' ([[W:Extended family|extended family]]) model in which biological kinship networks coexisted with cooperative institutions.

=== Urban and rural geography ===
{{Main|Social housing in Balisca}}

The PREC represented a shift in both the physical landscape and the social meaning of space. In urban areas, the expropriation of elite properties and their conversion to popular use established what contemporary observers termed the ''ciudad revolucionaria'' (revolutionary city), later being adopted internationally as the concept of the [[W:Right to the city|right to the city]]. Mansions would be converted communal housing or schools, or torn down and replaced by factories; while churches and other religious buildings would be repurposed as warehouses or community centers; vacant lots were transformed into urban gardens. The redistribution of urban space broke the geographic segregation that had characterized pre-revolutionary cities, establishing mixed-use, "mixed-class" communes that would persisted into later periods.
[[File:Los Espasios de Artesanos (1929).jpg|thumb|335x335px|[[Los Espasios de Artesanos]], a social housing project built in [[Castejón]] under the ambitious plans of the [[Alvaro Casadesús|Casadesús]] council ]]
The revolutionary emphasis on participatory planning reshaped urban development. Popular assemblies made decisions about land use, construction priorities, and infrastructure investment. Worker councils participated in the design of new housing, while residents' committees supervised maintenance and allocation. The ''barrio autogestionado'' (self-managed neighborhood) became a characteristic form, combining cooperative ownership with direct democratic governance.

In rural areas, [[W:Collectivization|collectivization]] would transform agricultural and industrial practices and the prevailing pattern of human settlement. The ''comuna agrícola'' ([[W:Collective farming|agricultural commune]]) and the related [[W:People's communes|people's communes]] (in the urban areas) replaced the hacienda as the basic unit of production; land was collectively owned and managed, while surplus was distributed according to cooperative principles. The consolidation of small holdings into larger units permitted mechanization and modernization, while the establishment of communal services—schools, clinics, cultural centers—in rural areas reduced the gap between city and countryside that had characterized pre-revolutionary Balisca.

The period witnessed significant construction of new housing and infrastructure. The ''Plan de Vivienda Popular'' (Popular Housing Plan), implemented between 1922 and 1934, funded the construction of over 3 million housing projects, much of it designed and built by worker cooperatives. These housing projects were notable for their massive footprints, each project effectively being it's own self-contained community: schools, clinics, and cultural facilities were integrated into each housing development, following an integrated approach to community development that has been a central tenent of Baliscan urban planning to the present day.

== Exclusions, contradictions and legacy ==

Despite the transformative aspirations of the PREC, certain populations remained excluded from full participation in the revolutionary project; the emigration of professionals would result in critical shortages of physicians, engineers, and administrators that the expanded educational system could not immediately fill, especially affecting rural communities. This "competence crisis" of the early 1920s prompted the revolutionary government to recruit foreign technical specialists—an arrangement that contradicted the anti-imperialist dimensions of revolutionary ideology.

Women's emancipation, while substantial, remained incomplete. The collectivization of domestic labor was implemented unevenly, with working-class women frequently retaining responsibility for household tasks even as they entered the formal workforce. The feminist organizations that had participated in the revolution found their autonomy increasingly constrained by the emphasis on unified popular struggle, generating tensions that would erupt in the feminist autonomist movements of the 1930s.

Indigenous decolonization, while rhetorically celebrated, encountered practical limitations. The restoration of land titles proceeded slowly in many regions, while the integration of indigenous governance into the confederal structure required compromises that some indigenous communities regarded as assimilationist. The pluralismo revolucionario framework resolved these tensions at the level of ideology, but material disputes over land, resources, and political authority persisted.

=== Legacy ===
{{See also|Socialism in Balisca|Libertarian socialism in Balisca}}

The social policies of the PREC established the foundation for contemporary Baliscan society. The secularization program, though modified in subsequent periods, permanently transformed the relationship between religious institutions and public life. Gender equality, while requiring continual reinforcement and extension, became a fundamental principle rather than a contested aspiration. Educational expansion created the literate, skilled population that enabled subsequent economic development. Indigenous autonomy, institutionalized during the PREC, remains a defining feature of the confederal structure.

The tensions and contradictions of the period continue to inform Baliscan political debate. The balance between individual liberty and collective obligation—the core problematic of libertarian socialism—remains unsettled, generating ongoing contestation over the proper scope of communal institutions. The relationship between universal emancipation and particular identities—whether indigenous, feminist, or queer—persists as a site of theoretical and practical struggle. The PREC thus remains not merely a historical period but a living reference point, invoked by revolutionaries and reformers alike as evidence of what transformation is possible and what challenges it entails.

